# In-container sshd. Runs as the unprivileged `dev` user on port 2222, which
# compose publishes on 127.0.0.1:2225 of zero.
#
# What it is FOR is different from or3-dev's, and the difference is the whole point
# of this container. or3-dev's sshd is also a tunnel endpoint — the phone terminates
# a reverse forward on it to reach the vessel LAN. This one is a DOOR AND NOTHING
# ELSE: you ssh in from the phone or a PC and work in an abduco session, and the
# model traffic that used to cross the phone's metered radio goes out over zero's
# home connection instead. Nothing tunnels back through here.
Port 2222
ListenAddress 0.0.0.0

# Persisted in the infra-ssh-host volume so a client's known_hosts entry survives a
# rebuild. Regenerating it every build would train you to accept a changed host key,
# which is the one habit that makes the check worthless.
HostKey /home/dev/.ssh-host/ssh_host_ed25519_key
PidFile /home/dev/.ssh-host/sshd.pid

# Key-only, `dev` only. A non-root sshd can only ever serve its own account.
AuthorizedKeysFile /home/dev/.ssh/authorized_keys
PubkeyAuthentication yes
PasswordAuthentication no
KbdInteractiveAuthentication no
PermitRootLogin no
UsePAM no                 # PAM needs root; a non-root sshd must disable it

# NO forwarding. or3-dev sets `AllowTcpForwarding yes` because a reverse forward is
# its reason to exist; nothing here needs one, and this container holds ssh keys that
# reach all three hosts as an account in `wheel`. Off is the smaller thing to defend,
# and turning it on should be a deliberate edit with a reason written next to it.
AllowTcpForwarding no
GatewayPorts no

# Let ssh sessions inherit the env the entrypoint writes to ~/.ssh/environment —
# ANSIBLE_CONFIG among it, which is what makes `ansible fleet -m ping` work from any
# directory in an ssh session rather than only from /workspace/ansible.
PermitUserEnvironment yes

# Compression, stated rather than inherited. `sshd -T` already reports `compression yes`
# on OpenSSH 9.2 — this changes no behaviour and is here because the behaviour is worth
# being able to see. It is also only half the switch: compression is negotiated, the ssh
# CLIENT defaults to `no`, and both ends have to offer it. The half that turns it on is
# in ansible/playbooks/dev-client.yml, on the aliases the phone uses.
#
# It is worth having on THIS session, which is not true of every sshd. or3's ssh_config
# has the argument: compression belongs on the session whose plaintext it is, because it
# happens before encryption, and ciphertext does not compress. Here the phone's ssh client
# terminates on this sshd — cloudflared is transport underneath, not another ssh session —
# so this IS that session, and what it compresses is claude's output on its way to a
# metered radio. or3's own sshd is the other case: it carries an inner session that is
# already ciphertext, and setting it there would buy nothing.
Compression yes

# Drop a wedged session rather than leaving one that accepts input and never answers:
# 3 x 30s of silence and it is gone. abduco is what makes that free — the work is not
# in the ssh session, so losing the session loses nothing.
ClientAliveInterval 30
ClientAliveCountMax 3

Subsystem sftp internal-sftp
StrictModes yes
